.TH VEC 5
.SH NAME
vec \- plain vectors of a drawing or a layer
.SH DESCRIPTION
A vec file is what a page or a layer draws, a record a line, in one
unit (mm for a board, points for a sheet), y up.
It is what the programs draw:
.IR schdraw (1)
writes a sheet and
.IR pcbvec (1)
the layers of a board;
.IR schview (1)
and
.IR pcbview (1)
show them and
.IR vecps (1)
prints them.
.PP
.RS
.nf
layer NAME              the layer of a board the file draws
dark | clear            the polarity of what follows
disk X Y R              a filled circle
line W X Y X Y ...      a stroke of width W, round at its ends;
                        of width 0, an outline, a hairline
poly X Y X Y ...        a filled polygon
text F S A J X Y TEXT   text, to the end of the line
page W H [N]            a page of a sheet: its size and number
name NAME X0 Y0 X1 Y1   a named box: a part, for picking
zone NET                the fill of a zone of copper begins
end                     and ends
part NAME [REF]         the records of a part begin
part                    and end
net NAME                the records of a net begin
net                     and end
pin REF.PIN X Y         a pin of a part: its end, or its pad's center
flag NET X Y port|global
                        the end of the pin of a flag of a net
# TEXT                  a comment
.fi
.RE
.PP
A file may hold several pages, each from its page record to the next;
the records before the first page record belong to the first page.
N is the number of the page, by its order in the source.
.PP
A layer record comes before any drawing and names the layer by KiCad's
name of it:
.BR F.Cu ,
.BR In1.Cu ,
\&...,
.B B.Cu
for copper,
.BR F.Mask ,
.BR F.Paste ,
.BR F.Silkscreen ,
.B F.Courtyard
(and
.B B.
on the bottom),
.B Edge.Cuts
for the outline and
.B Drill
for the holes; it is what a viewer shows the file as, whatever its
name.
Clear polarity erases what the file drew before, and nothing of other
files.
A text is in the font F (S, the stroke font; R, Nimbus Sans or
Helvetica; I, its italic), S high (for S, its capital height; else the
size of its em), turned A degrees counterclockwise about (X, Y), from
which it runs flush left (J 0), centered (1) or flush right (2); the
text is UTF-8.
The stroke font is the silkscreen's and the schematics': each printable
ASCII character on a grid 4 units wide, a unit a sixth of the capital
height, the capitals and digits 6 high, the lowercase 4 (ascenders 6),
the descenders 2 under the baseline, drawn at 0.88 of the grid's width
about its middle, each advancing 5 units, by a pen 0.15 of the capital
height wide, round at its ends (another character is drawn as ?).
The viewers draw R and I from the glyph table
lib/fonts/nimbussans.txt, the printer in the PostScript fonts; S both
draw stroke by stroke.
The records between
.B zone
and
.B end
are a zone's fill: its dark polygons give its outline, and what it
clears is its clearances.
The records between
.B part
.I NAME
and
.B part
draw that part, so a viewer can draw it brighter when it is selected;
a part may have several such runs.
The name is the part's in its source (outputs.ch1.gain.rf), which the viewers
select by; REF, its designator (R401), is for the reader.
A pin record gives where a wire meets a pin of a part on a sheet, or
the center of a pad of a pin on a layer of copper, by its designator
and pin number as the netlist names it (R401.2), and a flag
record where it meets a flag (a ground, a supply, a port), by the name
of the flag's net, and port for a port of the sheet or global for any
other flag (a ground, a supply, a global); they draw nothing, but say
what the drawing joins, so a checker can compare it with the netlist.
A flag is drawn between net records of its net, so it lights with the
net; a net bracket that holds a flag record is that flag's drawing, and
its lines are no wires.
The text records just before a flag record are the flag's text, a
record a span: a port's name, then each piece of its list of pages, the
bracket, each number and each comma, a text of its own; so a viewer or
a printer knows where each page number stands.
.SH SEE ALSO
gbrvec(1), vecps(1), pcbview(1), schview(1)
